NNAAMMEE
     cchhrroooott — change root directory

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     cchhrroooott [--GG _g_r_o_u_p[,,_g_r_o_u_p _._._.]] [--gg _g_r_o_u_p] [--uu _u_s_e_r] [--nn] _n_e_w_r_o_o_t
            [_c_o_m_m_a_n_d [_a_r_g _._._.]]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The cchhrroooott utility changes its current and root directories to the sup‐
     plied directory _n_e_w_r_o_o_t and then exec's _c_o_m_m_a_n_d with provided arguments,
     if supplied, or an interactive copy of the user's login shell.

     The options are as follows:

     --GG _g_r_o_u_p[,,_g_r_o_u_p _._._.]  Run the command with the permissions of the speci‐
                           fied groups.

     --gg _g_r_o_u_p              Run the command with the permissions of the speci‐
                           fied _g_r_o_u_p.

     --uu _u_s_e_r               Run the command as the _u_s_e_r.

     --nn                    Use the PROC_NO_NEW_PRIVS_CTL procctl(2) command
                           before chrooting, effectively disabling SUID/SGID
                           bits for the calling process and its descendants.
                           If security.bsd.unprivileged_chroot sysctl is set
                           to 1, it will make it possible to chroot without
                           superuser privileges.

EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT
     The following environment variable is referenced by cchhrroooott:

     SHELL  If set, the string specified by SHELL is interpreted as the name
            of the shell to exec.  If the variable SHELL is not set, _/_b_i_n_/_s_h
            is used.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     EExxaammppllee 11:: Chrooting into a New Root Directory

       The following command opens the csh(1) shell after chrooting to the
       standard root directory.

         # cchhrroooott // //bbiinn//ccsshh

     EExxaammppllee 22:: Execution of a Command with a Changed Root Directory

       The following command changes a root directory with cchhrroooott and then
       runs ls(1) to list the contents of _/_s_b_i_n.

         # cchhrroooott //ttmmpp//tteessttrroooott llss //ssbbiinn

